  "answer": "The implementation is complete. Here's a summary of what I did:\n\n## Changes to `ranges.mjs`\n\nI rewrote `mergeRanges(ranges)` to fix all the issues in the original:\n\n### 1. Input validation (`TypeError`)\n- Rejects non-array input.\n- Rejects any element that isn't a 2-element array.\n- Rejects bounds that aren't safe integers (`Number.isSafeInteger`).\n- Rejects `start > end`.\n\n### 2. No mutation of input\n- The original called `ranges.sort(...)` (in-place) and pushed references to the caller's pairs, then mutated them via `last[1] = ...`.\n- Now I build a **fresh** array of **fresh** pair copies (`.map(([start, end]) => [start, end])`) before sorting, so neither the input array nor any input pair is touched.\n\n### 3. Correct sorting\n- Sorts by start, then by end (`a[0] - b[0] || a[1] - b[1]`), as required.\n\n### 4. Adjacency merging\n- Changed the overlap test from `range[0] <= last[1]` to `range[0] <= last[1] + 1`, so touching integer intervals like `[2,4]` and `[5,7]` merge into `[2,7]`.\n\n### 5. Contained intervals don't shorten\n- Only extends the end when `range[1] > last[1]`, so a contained interval (e.g. `[3,4]` inside `[1,10]`) leaves the current interval intact.\n\n### 6. Fresh output\n- Returns a fresh result array containing fresh pair arrays; empty input returns `[]`.\n\nThe named export `mergeRanges` is preserved. Both provided tests (`unsorted overlaps merge in order` and `adjacent integer intervals touch`) pass with this logic.",
